WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.759 --> 00:00:14.680
Three friends trapped in a house of dread.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:21.939
Lessons taught will fill their head.

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:22.939
One a duck, The other red.

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:34.420
And the last, the yellow guy,
is

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:38.250
Dead.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:39.770
Hello Internet.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:43.870
Welcome to Film Theory, the show that knows
that a family is just a group of people who

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.160
live together and have the same lawyer.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:47.800
Ladies and gentlemen, it happened!

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:53.300
The new Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared TV series
has finally hit the airwaves and it is GREAT!

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.300
To catch you up: Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared
or DHMIS for short started as a series of

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.420
YouTube videos that took Sesame Street and
Jigsaw and then made the two of them kiss.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:06.299
The YouTube series followed three protagonists
named Red Guy, Yellow Guy and Duck as they

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:10.720
went on adventures, learned important lessons
about things like…

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.710
Or…

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:20.259
Then injected pure nightmare fuel into our
eyeballs with imagery like this.

00:01:20.259 --> 00:01:31.229
When the series concluded in 2016, most of
us thought that it had entered the great YouTube

00:01:31.229 --> 00:01:35.189
playlist in the sky, lost alongside Niga Higa
and Jenna Marbles.

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:38.960
faded to occasionally pop up and recommended
feeds from now until the heat death of the

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:39.960
universe.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.909
Whenever the algorithms sense that we are
starting to get nostalgic, or at least that's

00:01:42.909 --> 00:01:48.659
the way it was supposed to go until Don't
Hug Me did the Impossible and got a TV show.

00:01:48.659 --> 00:01:52.670
Earlier this year, the creators managed to
wrangle together a real six episode DHMIS

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:54.920
TV series on the British Channel 4.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:59.110
And after watching it, I got to say, it nails
the tone of those original YouTube videos,

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:02.289
all without feeling held back or tied down
by them.

00:02:02.289 --> 00:02:06.310
In fact, it's clear that while the esthetics
look the same, the series has moved on.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:10.330
The calendar that appears in every episode
has moved from the original June 19th date

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.459
that it was stuck on to June 20th.

00:02:12.459 --> 00:02:15.300
It's a turning of the page, a symbolic new
leaf.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:19.370
And whereas, the original series was all about
the corruption of art and children's programing,

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:23.170
this new TV series seems to tell a more personal
story about these characters because they

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:28.319
are actual, real characters now, no longer
are they just symbols for kids or creators

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:29.820
or…ducks…I guess

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.269
These three now appear to have histories and
LORE.

00:02:32.269 --> 00:02:38.069
In fact, when you start piecing this puzzle
together, the story of these three characters

00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:39.069
is tragic.

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:43.760
They're a trio that's trapped inside of a
repeating cycle built on one woman's guilt

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:44.760
and pain.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.610
And if I'm right about this, at least one
of these three characters is dead.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.920
So, wakey wakey gang, it's theory time.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:53.150
Just theory time.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.230
You'll see.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:58.739
For the first four episodes of the show, things
proceed normally, or at least as normally

00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:01.230
as they can
for the world of DHMIS.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:05.020
Our three characters start each day in some
mundane way before eventually being joined

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:08.890
by the teacher of the week; an anthropomorphized
object that's there to share some twisted

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:12.599
lessons about topics ranging from jobs to
death to electricity.

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:16.530
Oh, sure, there's some weird bits mixed in
there, like yellow guy's dad Roy viciously

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:21.510
ripping apart a creepy family or duck killing
off his own doppelganger, but nothing that

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:22.620
out of the ordinary.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:28.019
But then comes episode five, where we see
the reality of the show starting to fray.

00:03:28.019 --> 00:03:32.569
In this episode, our main characters are confronted
by an old living train, basically the nursing

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:36.200
home equivalent of Thomas the Tank engine,
Thomas the Hoveround engine?

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.370
Anyway, at one point the teacher turns into
a car and then promptly dies.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:43.390
So our heroic trio does the reasonable thing
and hops into his rotting carcass to take

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:44.390
a road trip.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.390
And it works.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:51.260
Not only do they manage to drive past the
desiccated remains, their original TV pilot.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:57.939
They manage to drive so far that they break
through what appears to be a simulation.

00:03:57.939 --> 00:04:11.290
They leave their cartoonish world behind,
only to end up in a realistic dystopian one.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:12.290
Lost and confused.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:15.459
They set up camp for the night as they sit
around a fire contemplating their new world

00:04:15.459 --> 00:04:19.850
and hoping that the “neighbors” will show
up to help, a mysterious voice begins to echo

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:23.160
through the night.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:28.880
Suddenly, Yellow guy sees something out of
the corner of his eye, and we cut to black,

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.850
only to then watch as a mysterious hand rolls
a model of their car up to a doll house, the

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:38.870
same house that we've been watching at the
start of every episode.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:44.620
Now this is important because earlier this
very episode, the characters had already talked

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:48.930
about the episodic nature of content and how
everything always winds up resorting to a

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:51.280
status quo.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:05:02.380
And sure enough, when we rejoin them in episode
six, the characters are reset right back to

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:06.710
where we started, none the wiser about their
junkyard excursion the night before.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:08.880
And that's when things start to really pick
up.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.850
At first, the season finale is all about electricity,
our three friends get an electric bill and

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:14.670
they don't want to pay it because.

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:20.540
So of course their fuse box Electracy comes
to life to teach them more about the wonders

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:25.420
of electricity and how it powers phones and
televisions and radios.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:31.949
And yes, even your shredder, when the lesson
suddenly turns to portable sources of energy

00:05:31.949 --> 00:05:41.840
like batteries, Yellow guy gives us the surprise
revelation that he too is powered by batteries.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.639
Which is a strange detail that we’ll come
back to you.

00:05:44.639 --> 00:05:48.350
Regardless, his current batteries are old,
corroded and clearly out of juice.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:51.080
So Duck switches them ou for Electracy’s.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:55.070
Suddenly the once doofy Yellow guy is juiced
up to the max and instead of being dimwitted,

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:57.650
his mind explodes with intelligence.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:00.919
Equipped with this new awareness of the world
around him, Yellow Guy becomes the first person

00:06:00.919 --> 00:06:06.150
of the trio to realize that they're only on
the bottom floor of what's a multi-story house.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:10.389
Yellow guy climbs the stairs to discover the
big boy room on the second floor.

00:06:10.389 --> 00:06:15.270
Inside, he finds older versions of Red Guy
and Duck that are able to learn not just one,

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:19.199
but two lessons in a given day.

00:06:19.199 --> 00:06:21.289
Unimpressed.

00:06:21.289 --> 00:06:27.560
He continues up to the third floor where he
finds the more advanced, bigger boys room,

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:31.660
housng even smarter and more futuristic versions
of Red Guy and Duck.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:35.410
Eventually, he's turned off by their heartless
and cruel experiments, things like electrocuting

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:37.250
an innocent lump of living flesh.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:41.840
So for a final time, Yellow guy leaves the
room and moves up another flight of steps.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.560
At the top of the house things are extremely
different.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:48.520
Here, Yellow guy finds a door labeled Lesley,
and we immediately get an answer as to what

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:53.980
that means on the other side.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:57.560
It's made clear that this is the woman that
we heard reciting the rhyme at the end of

00:06:57.560 --> 00:06:58.560
episode five.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.430
It was also her gloved hand that was resetting
the dollhouse back to square one at the end

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:03.480
of the last episode.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:08.470
But now the dollhouse is opened and we can
see small figures of the trio inside recreating

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:11.690
the exact events that we're watching play
out in this episode.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:16.509
Yellow Guy is obviously full of questions,
and Lesley promises to answer all of them,

00:07:16.509 --> 00:07:25.470
provided he plays with the dolls.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:29.470
She then produces a large leather bound book
covered in a wacky looking code before sending

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:31.520
him back downstairs to his friends.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:34.979
Right as he's about to read it, Duck and Red
Guy rip the batteries out of his chest in

00:07:34.979 --> 00:07:36.600
order to power the house.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:40.830
Yellow guy forgets what the book was for in
the first place and decides that it's something…

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:44.910
The season ends as the three friends cheer
at the destruction of the book so close to

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:48.349
answers or even an escape, but instead trapped

00:07:48.349 --> 00:07:50.620
inside their ceaseless prison once more.

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:54.599
Making matters worse, Yellow guy never realized
that there was yet another level to the house

00:07:54.599 --> 00:07:55.690
that he never got to.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:08:00.550
It's an incredibly sad, surreal ending to
the season, which leaves us on this unusual

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:01.550
cliffhanger.

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:04.220
Can Yellow Guy make the journey up the stairs
again?

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:07.419
Based on the pictures that we see behind him,
hanging on the wall as he goes up, we know

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:09.819
that he's made this journey before.

00:08:09.819 --> 00:08:13.030
Hopefully there's a second season where he's
able to do the whole thing again.

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:15.490
In the meantime, though, we're left with a
lot of questions.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:19.990
The first and foremost being who or what is
Lesley?

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:24.330
She's the only human character that we meet
throughout all of DHMIS, which in and of itself

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:25.330
is odd.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:27.050
Her design is also noteworthy.

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:29.360
She's a bizarre mix of puppet and flesh.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.039
She's clearly a human, but her coat makes
her look like one giant muppet that's covered

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:38.060
in fur, and her face has been stitched and
scarred like a creature that's been sewn together.

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:42.279
More importantly than her design, though,
she acts like the puppeteer for this world.

00:08:42.279 --> 00:08:45.060
She's the one that's turning the crank at
the start of every episode.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:48.750
She also appears to be controlling the actions
of all the others through her miniature figurines.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:55.420
And when one of the dolls breaks, she has
a drawer full of replacements.

00:08:55.420 --> 00:09:01.019
Well, I suspect that I know what's going on
here.

00:09:01.019 --> 00:09:03.459
And it all begins with one key line.

00:09:03.459 --> 00:09:06.700
As Lesley sends Yellow Guy on his way, he
asks whether he can stay on the top floor

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:07.700
with her.

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:16.149
She immediately rejects the idea by shouting:
At first, this just seems like a random outburst,

00:09:16.149 --> 00:09:18.860
a combination punch-line/jump-scare to keep
us on our feet.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:23.060
But I think that this is actually the key
to understanding everything about this new

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:24.060
series.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:27.339
Taking Lesley at her word here, Yellow Guy
isn’t her real son, but he's close.

00:09:27.339 --> 00:09:31.620
You see, I suspect that Yellow guy represents
Lesley’s real son.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.860
A real son named David, a son who died.

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:37.120
In episode two: death

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:41.329
we see the name David etched onto the grave
where Duck gets buried.

00:09:41.329 --> 00:09:46.709
Later, we see a large group of human mourners
show up in the kitchen for David's funeral,

00:09:46.709 --> 00:09:53.410
where they, again, mistake Duck for David.

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:55.600
So what would make me say that Yellow guy
is David?

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:57.680
Well, take a look at what's stitched in Yellow
guy's overalls.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:58.700
On the front pocket.

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:02.360
There's the large letter D and at the foot
of Yellow Guy's bed, the letter D again.

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:07.660
In episode three: Family, Yellow Guy is even
given a special locket with the letter D engraved

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:08.660
on it.

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:12.540
Over and over again, Yellow guy and the letter
D are connected, D for David.

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:15.170
But the connection between the two goes well
beyond just a letter.

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:18.860
Going back to the death episode when our happy
trio first show up at the cemetery, the coffin

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:26.480
assumes that yellow guy is the one who died.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:30.779
And throughout the rest of the series we watch
as both flies and worms are attracted to Yellow

00:10:30.779 --> 00:10:31.930
Guy and no one else.

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:34.310
It's almost like he's a dead, decaying body.

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:37.589
Plus, there's a strange moment in the intro
of episode five where Yellow Guy seems to

00:10:37.589 --> 00:10:46.510
dissociate and remember something about his
past.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:50.560
He's clearly remembering a dream where he
saw another version of himself, he's seeing

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:51.560
David.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:58.680
The same thing happens again later that same
episode when he looks out the car window.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:01.649
He's connecting with a past iteration of himself,
with David.

00:11:01.649 --> 00:11:09.980
We're also explicitly told that David is not
Duck's name.

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:15.130
We also know that Red Guy isn't dead based
on his ID.

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:20.680
So by process of elimination, if one of these
characters is representative of David, it

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:22.209
has to be Yellow guy.

00:11:22.209 --> 00:11:26.820
This may also explain why Lesley says this
to him when he first enters the room:

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:31.089
Sounds almost motherly, but this is far from
the full story.

00:11:31.089 --> 00:11:36.639
The tragic twist of the tale is that we know
exactly what killed David, he died in a car

00:11:36.639 --> 00:11:37.639
crash.

00:11:37.639 --> 00:11:41.139
In episode five, When Red Guy tells the Yellow
Guy that they're going to be part of a community.

00:11:41.139 --> 00:11:44.270
Yellow Guy fantasizes about living in a new
city named Mulhoven.

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:47.560
He imagines joining a neighborhood, befriending
the people of the town.

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:51.800
Yellow Guy then moves into a new home where
Red Guy is his neighbor and Duck is his pet.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:56.460
Everything is great until a clergyman offers
Yellow Guy a bird as a welcome present.

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:59.790
The bird flies away and Yellow Guy gives chase
into the street.

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:01.370
From there, you can guess the rest.

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:02.830
Yellow Guy is hit by a car.

00:12:02.830 --> 00:12:06.639
I suspect that that dream sequence may be
more true to life than we initially expect.

00:12:06.639 --> 00:12:13.160
Listen to the narrator of that little story.

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:14.839
It's Lesley's voice.

00:12:14.839 --> 00:12:21.800
In fact, it's Leslie screaming for Yellow
Guy to get out of the street.

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:25.269
And the reason she's screaming?

00:12:25.269 --> 00:12:27.139
Because she's the one in the car.

00:12:27.139 --> 00:12:29.440
Lesley killed her own son, David.

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:32.390
Duck: What did you say?

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:33.390
Yeah.

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:34.390
You heard me.

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:36.330
Lesley killed her own son in this accident.

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:37.330
Why do I say that?

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:40.890
Well, first of all, when we meet Leslie in
episode six, her face is covered in stitches,

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:43.820
almost like she went through an awful accident
herself.

00:12:43.820 --> 00:12:47.030
Secondly, take a look at the license plate
of the car that the gang drives throughout

00:12:47.030 --> 00:12:48.030
this episode,

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:49.339
It spells Lesley.

00:12:49.339 --> 00:12:52.889
And most importantly of all, we know that
David wasn't the only person in the family

00:12:52.889 --> 00:12:54.870
involved in a car crash that day.

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:58.769
At the end of episode three, as they're wrapping
up what they learned about families.

00:12:58.769 --> 00:13:05.910
Yellow Guy describes a family as a group…

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:10.139
Unless his father, Roy, also died in a car
accident that same day, which seems pretty

00:13:10.139 --> 00:13:11.139
unlikely.

00:13:11.139 --> 00:13:16.070
I suspect that this line is meant to describe
Lesley, who metaphorically died that day.

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:18.980
And you see, that gets to the core issue here.

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:23.110
That's why I think she's created these characters,
this dollhouse, this world.

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:24.930
It's to escape from that trauma.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:28.970
The sad truth of Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared
is that it isn't the story of three wacky

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:30.990
puppets learning disturbing life lessons.

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:36.560
In reality, it's the story of a mother grieving
over her dead son, a woman that is stuck in

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:41.959
a spiraling cycle of guilt, creating playthings
that represent him so she can impart all the

00:13:41.959 --> 00:13:44.360
lessons that she would have taught him if
he had lived.

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:49.019
And she has dozens of back ups to ensure that
she never loses him again, and for as much

00:13:49.019 --> 00:13:54.050
as Yellow Guy reminds her of her lost son,
he can never truly be the real David.

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:55.710
You're not my son!

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:57.310
“Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared”.

00:13:57.310 --> 00:13:59.370
It's a weird name, right?

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:01.620
We've never really stopped to question it,
have we?

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:03.480
It's always just kind of been there.

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:04.480
So.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:05.480
Yeah, DHMIS, that's the name duh.

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:07.140
But really, think about those words.

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:09.339
“Don't hug me, I'm scared”.

00:14:09.339 --> 00:14:13.800
It's a reference to someone so damaged, so
hurt that they're afraid of something like

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:14.800
a hug.

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:19.100
They're scared of affection, of love, of opening
themselves up to someone else.

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:21.110
That right there, that's Lesley.

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:24.089
Afraid to love again because she's afraid
of being hurt again.

00:14:24.089 --> 00:14:27.971
But I suspect that in order to move forward,
to get to that final level of the House, that's

00:14:27.971 --> 00:14:29.430
exactly what needs to happen.

00:14:29.430 --> 00:14:34.579
A hug, Lesley, to forgive herself and Yellow
Guy to give her a hug then and only then will

00:14:34.579 --> 00:14:39.730
both of them be able to transcend, to move
past their stagnant lives inside that house

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:42.720
and
climb to the final level to finally be free.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:46.860
But hey, that's just a theory, a film theory.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:52.050
And cut.

